From 45d59391b3c3715e5f3046219e4bd8891e7a7898 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: suiyan <suiyan@sunmmio.com>
Date: Tue, 14 Jul 2026 11:56:29 +0800
Subject: [PATCH 28/29] target/dadao: fix double-offset memory corruption in
 applyFixup (ML-003i)
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

DS's gdb session (ML-003i) found real evidence of MC fragment memory
corruption: MCFragment::Parent and STI overwritten with the identical
garbage value 0x8802000000000000, crashing MCAssembler::layout() with
a bare SIGSEGV (no assertion — a real buffer overrun, not a caught
invariant violation).

Root cause: DADAOAsmBackend::applyFixup's `Data` parameter already
points at this fixup's location within the fragment (the caller
pre-offsets it) — confirmed against RISC-V's RISCVAsmBackend::
applyFixup, which indexes `Data[Idx]` directly and asserts
`Fixup.getOffset() + NumBytes <= F.getSize()` rather than adding the
offset again. DADAO's FK_Data_8 case (added in ML-003e) and the
generic fallback case wrote to `Data + Offset` — double-applying the
offset — writing past the intended bytes into whatever memory follows,
which is exactly how an adjacent MCFragment's own struct fields
(Parent/STI) got clobbered.

This was invisible in every prior test: earlier tests only ever had a
single fixup per (small) fragment, where Offset happened to be 0 in
practice. A jump table is the first case with *multiple* FK_Data_8
fixups packed into one larger shared fragment at different non-zero
offsets — the double-offset bug finally had daylight to write out of
bounds.

(An earlier attempt in this same debugging session went the opposite,
wrong direction — added `+ Offset` to the call24/branch18/branch12/
rela_page cases, which were *already correct* without it; this
regressed clang_oneshot.test with an ArrayRef::slice out-of-bounds
assertion and was reverted.)

Verified: the crash is gone (vfprintf.c with real -ffunction-sections
flags now fails cleanly with "Undefined temporary symbol" instead of
SIGSEGV — a separate, still-open, non-crashing issue, likely needing
ELFObjectWriter::useSectionSymbol / needsRelocateWithSymbol to route
local/temporary jump-table-index labels through section-relative
relocations). E2E 27/27, differential AGREE(4-way)=200/DIVERGE=0.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 4.8 <noreply@anthropic.com>
---
 .../Target/DADAO/MCTargetDesc/DADAOAsmBackend.cpp   | 13 ++++++++++---
 1 file changed, 10 insertions(+), 3 deletions(-)

diff --git a/llvm/lib/Target/DADAO/MCTargetDesc/DADAOAsmBackend.cpp b/llvm/lib/Target/DADAO/MCTargetDesc/DADAOAsmBackend.cpp
index 4488e6fda503..095324492fa6 100644
--- a/llvm/lib/Target/DADAO/MCTargetDesc/DADAOAsmBackend.cpp
+++ b/llvm/lib/Target/DADAO/MCTargetDesc/DADAOAsmBackend.cpp
@@ -31,7 +31,14 @@ public:
   void applyFixup(const MCFragment &F, const MCFixup &Fixup,
                   const MCValue &Target, uint8_t *Data, uint64_t Value,
                   bool IsResolved) override {
-    unsigned Offset = Fixup.getOffset();
+    // NOTE: Data already points at this fixup's location within the
+    // fragment (the caller pre-offsets it) — don't add Fixup.getOffset()
+    // again when indexing into Data. Adding it a second time (as a prior
+    // version of this file did for FK_Data_8 / the generic fallback) wrote
+    // past the intended bytes and corrupted adjacent MC fragment data —
+    // only surfaced once a single fragment held multiple fixups at
+    // different offsets (e.g. a multi-entry jump table), never in earlier
+    // one-fixup-per-fragment tests (ML-003i).
     unsigned Kind = Fixup.getKind();
 
     if (Kind == DADAO::fixup_dadao_call24) {
@@ -124,12 +131,12 @@ public:
         maybeAddReloc(F, Fixup, Target, Value, IsResolved);
         return;
       }
-      support::endian::write64be(Data + Offset, Value);
+      support::endian::write64be(Data, Value);
       return;
     }
 
     if (IsResolved)
-      support::endian::write32be(Data + Offset,
+      support::endian::write32be(Data,
                                   static_cast<uint32_t>(Value) & 0xFFFFFFFF);
   }
 
